7. Rules for using the AI builder
Give it the real route table. Never let it invent stops or times.
Tell it: "Calculations must be done in code, the LLM only explains results."
Tell it: "Every result shows a label: scheduled / reported / estimated / unknown."
Test what it builds. Looking finished is not the same as being correct.
If something is not working by the freeze, cut it. Don't half-ship.
8. Must-test before demo
 Zero reports
 One fresh report
 One stale report
 Two conflicting reports
 AI turned off (results screen still works)
 Works on a phone-sized screen
 The 3 scenarios give sensible answers vs. the hand-worked examples
9. Demo script (aim for under 5 minutes)
Open on a phone-sized view. Pick a stop + destination.
Show the normal scheduled route info.
Trigger "point already left my stop."
Show the "board at a later stop" option with its labels and assumptions.
Submit a report. Show its timestamp and status.
Show the zero-report state: the app says "unknown," not a fake time.
Show the AI explanation.
Close with the roadmap slide (live tracking, learned ETAs, native app).

Judging: Problem & impact 30% | Scope & buildability 30% | Spec quality 25% | Team readiness 15%.

10. Product statement (use in README and pitch)

OnRoute helps NED students recover when their usual university shuttle plan fails. It combines verified route information, recent student observations, and available travel estimates to compare realistic next steps. It does not claim live bus tracking or certainty where the data does not support it.

Guiding rule: a small reliable flow that works beats many features that don't.